From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: omp <noreply@example.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Skip rpath flags for static PETSc

distutils turns ``runtime_library_dirs`` into ``-Wl,--enable-new-dtags
-Wl,-rpath,<dir>``. Under emscripten the compiler is ``emcc`` and the
linker step is ``wasm-ld``, which rejects ``--enable-new-dtags``:

    wasm-ld: error: unknown argument: --enable-new-dtags

The guard upstream uses is ``sys.platform != 'win32'``, but under
cross-python ``sys.platform`` is ``unknown`` (the host interpreter reports
the build machine), so it does not trigger. ``sysconfig.get_platform()``
here is ``unknown-wasm32``, and there is no ``sys.platform`` value for
emscripten at all.

An rpath is meaningless for this build anyway: PETSc is installed static
(``BUILDSHAREDLIB = no``) and the extension modules are emscripten side
modules, which do not resolve libraries through the dynamic loader. So
gate the rpath block on PETSc actually being shared, which is also correct
for the non-emscripten static case.

--- a/src/binding/petsc4py/conf/confpetsc.py
+++ b/src/binding/petsc4py/conf/confpetsc.py
@@ -317,8 +317,10 @@
             '-L{} {}'.format(self['PETSC_LIB_DIR'], self['PETSC_LIB_BASIC']),
         )
         petsc_lib = flaglist(lib_flags)
-        # runtime_library_dirs is not supported on Windows
-        if sys.platform != 'win32':
+        # runtime_library_dirs is not supported on Windows, and it is
+        # pointless for static PETSc: distutils would pass
+        # -Wl,--enable-new-dtags, which wasm-ld rejects.
+        if sys.platform != 'win32' and self['BUILDSHAREDLIB'] == 'yes':
             # if DESTDIR is set, then we're building against PETSc in a staging
             # directory, but rpath needs to point to final install directory.
             rpath = [strip_prefix(self.DESTDIR, self['PETSC_LIB_DIR'])]
